</ul>
<p>These channels help you explore the field and build foundational knowledge. For job-ready skills, structured training with projects and placement support remains the faster path.</p>
<h2><strong>Ideal Learning Pathways: Your Roadmap Based on Where You Are Right Now</strong></h2>
<p>You do not need a computer science degree to become a data scientist. What you need is a structured plan that maps your existing experience to the right learning sequence.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<h3><strong>Fresh Graduates</strong></h3>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with Python, SQL, and statistics. These three skills form the foundation. Build two to three portfolio projects on GitHub using real datasets. Target entry-level data analyst roles first. You can transition into a data scientist role within one to two years of hands-on experience.</p>
<p>A structured program like the AnalytixLabs Data Science 360 course or the PG in Data Science covers this entire progression in one curriculum.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<h3><strong>Professionals from IT, Analytics, or Engineering</strong></h3>
</li>
</ul>
<p>You already have technical foundations. Focus your upskilling on machine learning, advanced statistics, and domain-specific applications. With targeted effort over six to ten months, you can move into mid-level data scientist roles directly.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<h3><strong>Professionals from Non-Technical Domains</strong></h3>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If you come from marketing, operations, finance, or a similar background, start with data analytics. Build your data literacy with Excel, SQL, and Power BI first. Then layer Python, statistics, and ML on top. The <a href="https://www.analytixlabs.co.in/course/data-analytics/">AnalytixLabs Data Analytics course</a> works well as a stepping stone here. Expect a timeline of ten to fourteen months.</p>
<p><strong>➤ Not sure which path fits you? Talk to an <a href="https://www.analytixlabs.co.in/contact/">AnalytixLabs career counsellor</a> to map your next steps.</strong></p>
<h2><strong>Conclusion</strong></h2>
<p>Hyderabad’s data science job market is growing faster than any other Indian city. The combination of GCC expansion, government investment in AI infrastructure, a deep pharma and BFSI employer base, and lower living costs creates a strong foundation for long-term career growth. The skills employers need are clear. The sectors hiring are diverse. The salary ranges are competitive. The question is whether you are ready.</p>
<p>Start building your skills today. The market is hiring, and the window for early-mover advantage in Hyderabad is wide open.</p>
<h2><strong>Frequently Asked Questions</strong></h2>
<h3><strong>How Do You Get a Data Scientist Job in Hyderabad as a Fresher?</strong></h3>
<p>Build your foundations in Python, SQL, and machine learning first. Complete two to three portfolio projects that demonstrate your ability to clean data, build models, and communicate insights. Apply through Naukri, LinkedIn, and company career pages. A structured certification from a recognised institute strengthens your profile, especially when recruiters receive 500+ applications per role.</p>
<h3><strong>What Is the Average Data Scientist Salary in Hyderabad in 2026?</strong></h3>
<p>The average falls between 12 and 14.5 LPA, depending on the source. Glassdoor reports 13.5 LPA as the mean, with a typical range of 7.87 to 19 LPA. Top earners at MNCs can reach 28+ LPA. Total compensation at top-tier companies like Salesforce averages over 56 LPA according to Levels.fyi.</p>
<h3><strong>Is Hyderabad Better Than Bengaluru for Data Science Jobs?</strong></h3>
<p>It depends on what you optimize for. Bengaluru has more total listings and a larger startup ecosystem. Hyderabad is the fastest-growing GCC hub with comparable MNC salaries and significantly lower living costs. If you want the best salary-to-cost ratio with strong long-term job growth, Hyderabad is a compelling choice.</p>
<h3><strong>Which Sectors Hire the Most Data Scientists in Hyderabad?</strong></h3>
<p>IT services and GCCs lead with roughly 63% of postings. Healthcare and life sciences follow at about 8.6%, driven by Hyderabad’s pharma infrastructure. BFSI accounts for 5.4%. Emerging sectors like semiconductors, aerospace, and automotive are smaller in volume but growing fast.</p>
<h3><strong>Do You Need a Degree in Computer Science to Become a Data Scientist?</strong></h3>
<p>No. Many working data scientists hold degrees in mathematics, statistics, economics, engineering, and even non-technical fields. What matters is demonstrable skill in Python, SQL, statistics, and machine learning. A strong portfolio backed by practical projects carries more weight than a specific degree title.</p>
<h3><strong>What Are the Best Free Resources to Learn Data Science in India?</strong></h3>
<p>YouTube channels like StatQuest, 3Blue1Brown, Krish Naik, and codebasics offer excellent free content. Kaggle provides practice datasets and competitions. Google’s ML Crash Course is another solid starting point. For job-ready, placement-supported training, explore structured programs from institutes like AnalytixLabs.</p>
