<td>Productionalisation, cost/scale optimisation (premium due to development of enterprise-grade applications)</td>
<td>₹15-26 LPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>GenAI / LLM skills</strong></td>
<td>Fine-tuning, prompt engineering, agentic AI, product integration (high demand and niche skills allow for higher premiums)</td>
<td>₹22-40 LPA</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3><strong>3. Industry</strong></h3>
<p>Different sectors offer distinct compensation bands based on data volume and business stakes. The variance stems from how critical analytics is to each industry’s core value chain.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Product and Tech Companies</strong>: Firms in SaaS, AI, and digital products typically offer the highest salary of data scientist in India due to data-centric models and innovation budgets.</li>
<li><strong>BFSI (Banking, Financial Services &#x26; Insurance)</strong>: Roles in risk modeling, fraud detection, and algorithmic trading attract strong pay due to regulatory complexity and financial exposure.</li>
<li><strong>IT Services and Consulting</strong>: Offer consistent but moderate salaries with opportunities to work across multiple client domains.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Industry</strong></th>
<th><strong>Nature of Job and Factors that Affect Remuneration</strong></th>
<th><strong>Average salary</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>IT Services / Consulting</strong></td>
<td>Volume hiring and client-billable roles; steadier but generally lower salary base</td>
<td>₹15 LPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>BFSI / Fintech</strong></td>
<td>High-stakes model creation (risk, fraud); Premium pay due to direct revenue/risk impact of data science solutions</td>
<td>₹17.5 LPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Product / SaaS companies</strong></td>
<td>Develops data-driven features and applications; deep involvement in data science and high investment in it attracts top-tier pay</td>
<td>₹18.5 LPA</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3><strong>4. Company Type</strong></h3>
<p>Apart from the industry, the company size and business maturity also play a pivotal role in explaining the salary variation.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>MNCs and Tech Giants</strong>: Such companies offer structured career paths, stability, and global-scale data systems. Therefore, they have higher pay bands and additional perks such as bonuses and stock options.</li>
<li><strong>Startups</strong>: Often trade fixed pay for growth potential or equity. This typically appeals to candidates seeking rapid learning or future upside. While exposure is much more in startups, the variable component of the salary is generally higher, which is tied to the funding stages or equity. Another thing to note is that the salary range is typically much wider compared to other industries.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th><strong>Company Type</strong></th>
<th><strong>Nature of Job and Factors that Affect Remuneration</strong></th>
<th><strong>Average salary</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>MNCs / Tech Giants</strong></td>
<td>Data Science plays a critical role, with large budgets being allocated to dedicated data science departments involved in the creation of software products, thereby providing high data science salaries</td>
<td>₹18 LPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Startups / Unicorns</strong></td>
<td>Extremely varied nature of work with the role of data science being extremely limited to critical; this causes a wide variance with the structure of remuneration ranging from lower fixed pay + equity (early-stage) to top pay (well-funded)</td>
<td>Extremely wide range; it depends on the technology involved</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3><strong>5. Location</strong></h3>
<p>Geography is a major defining factor in Indian salary structures. Tech hubs such as Bengaluru, Gurgaon, and Hyderabad offer the highest compensation due to dense startup ecosystems and MNC presence. These regions host large clusters of AI and analytics firms competing for limited senior talent. Conversely, Tier-2 cities like Ahmedabad or Jaipur offer lower pay but are offset by the lower cost of living. This pattern is not unique to India and mirrors global trends where metropolises pay a premium due to proximity to other innovation centres.</p>
